  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 110 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nSynopsis: Moisture will continue to work back into southern \nWisconsin Tuesday, leading to muggier conditions. A cold front will \nswing through the western Great Lakes Tuesday evening into \nWednesday, triggering the next round of shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment. Tuesday evening & night continues to be most favored \nfor storms in southern Wisconsin. Broader ingredients will be in \nplace for a few stronger storms with heavy downpours, though trends \ncontinue to be monitored for more precise placement & intensity \ndetails. The cold front is forecast to be south of the Wisconsin-\nIllinois border by Wednesday morning, with drier northerly flow & \npassing high pressure leading to pleasant & precip-free conditions \nWednesday afternoon through Thursday. Winds will trend southwesterly \non Friday, bringing milder and modestly more humid air to the \nregion. The next cold front is forecast to move through the western \nGreat Lakes Friday evening, when some scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible. Pleasant conditions will return by \nthe Saturday-Sunday time frame behind the departed front.  \n\nTuesday Evening: Continue to anticipate a window for scattered \nshowers and storms between ~3 PM & Midnight along the approaching \ncold front. Broad setup details---particularly forcing from the \nfront, relatively favorable arrival timing of the frontal forcing, \nand sufficient wind shear---point toward an environment generally \nfavorable for more organized storm development in southern \nWisconsin. The limiting factor, however, remains the quality of \nmoisture return (& thus instability) ahead of the encroaching \nfront/on the back side of departing high pressure. With a source \nregion centered well to the west over South Dakota/Nebraska, \nreturning moisture will be racing the cold front to southern \nWisconsin, with any combination of a faster frontal speed and/or \nslower-departing high impeding its progress. This will be the main \ntrend being monitored over the next 24-36 hours. A stronger storm \nwith gusty winds would be possible wherever the deepest (65+ degF) \nmoisture returns can establish, with current guidance suggesting the \ngreatest potential from Madison & points southwest. Seasonably moist \nconditions will support heavy downpours in any storms, though \nflooding potential would be low in the absence of any training.  \n\nQuigley",
